      "s": "TechCrunch",
      "t": "Judge allows NAACP lawsuit over xAI data center pollution to move forward",
      "d": "An NAACP lawsuit accuses the SpaceX-owned AI company of violating the Clean Air Act.",
      "u": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/31/judge-allows-naacp-lawsuit-over-xai-data-center-pollution-to-move-forward/",
      "m": "10. 31. PM 09:17",
      "ts": 1793481423000,
      "fullText": "A federal judge denied a motion by xAI seeking to dismiss an NAACP lawsuit accusing the SpaceX-owned company of failing to obtain permits needed to operate the gas turbines powering its Colossus data center outside Memphis. Suing on behalf of the NAACP, the Southern Environmental Law Center said the AI company had “installed and operated at least 35 combustion turbines and other sources of air pollution at the Colossus site without ever obtaining the necessary preconstruction or operating air permits.” It also accused xAI of violating the Clean Air Act. xAI sought to have the lawsuit dismissed, claiming that the turbines are exempt from permitting requirements because they’re mobile. The company also argued that the plaintiffs did not have the standing to sue. “Because the plaintiffs have statutory standing under the Clean Air Act, because the combustion turbines are not ‘mobile sources’ under Mississippi’s [State Implementation Plan], and because the citizen suit provision does not violate Article II of the Constitution or the private nondelegation doctrine, the plaintiffs’ claims will proceed,” Judge Debra M. Brown wrote in her decision . Focusing on xAI’s claim that these kinds of citizen lawsuits are unconstitutional, Judge Brown wrote, “Because citizen suit plaintiffs sue on their own behalf, in their own name, and seek penalties that redress harm to themselves, they are not exercising the federal government’s executive power.” TechCrunch has reached out to SpaceX for comment. In a statement , the NAACP’s director of environmental and climate justice Abre’ Connor described this as “another important victory in our effort to hold xAI accountable.” (A similar motion to dismiss from the Department of Justice was denied earlier this month ) “The courts have repeatedly rejected attempts by xAI and the DOJ to dismiss the NAACP’s Clean Air Act case, affirming our right to pursue justice for communities impacted by pollution,” Connor said. “Our position has been clear:"
    },
    {
      "s": "TechCrunch",
      "t": "Salesforce’s M&#038;A spree continues with a reported $375M deal for defense tech startup Rune",
      "d": "The AI logistics startup will bolster the CRM giant's Missionforce defense platform.",
      "u": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/30/salesforces-ma-spree-continues-with-a-reported-375m-deal-for-defense-tech-startup-rune/",
      "m": "10. 30. PM 11:21",
      "ts": 1793402483000,
      "fullText": "In Brief Posted: 4:21 PM PDT · October 30, 2026 Image Credits: David Paul Morris/Bloomberg / Getty Images Marina Temkin Salesforce’s M&A spree continues with a reported $375M deal for defense tech startup Rune Salesforce has agreed to acquire Rune Technologies, a startup that builds AI software to help the U.S. military predict supply needs and manage logistics from the field, for $375 million in cash and stock, Resilience Media reported . The acquisition underscores Salesforce’s push into defense tech through Missionforce, its business unit serving national security and defense agencies. Since Missionforce launched last year, its business has grown 80% year over year, driven in part by a new 10-year, $5.6 billion contract with the U.S. Army, the company said in a blog post . By integrating Rune’s frontline logistics data, Missionforce will extend beyond back-office administrative planning to support battlefield operations. Since its founding in 2024, Rune has raised a total of $30 million from investors, including Andreessen Horowitz, Point72 Ventures, and IQT, a CIA-backed nonprofit venture firm formerly known as In-Q-Tel. The startup was last valued at $127 million when it raised a Series A led by Human Capital last year, according to PitchBook. Salesforce has been on an acquisition spree this year. In addition to Rune, the CRM giant agreed to buy AI agent platform Fin (formerly Intercom) for $3.6 billion, bought Qualified for $1.2 billion, and signed an agreement to buy Listen Labs after the startup walked away from a $125 million Series C term sheet at a $1.5 billion valuation, as TechCrunch reported . Salesforce didn&#8217;t respond to a request for comment. Topics defense tech , Enterprise , In Brief , Mergers and Acquisitions , Rune technologies , Salesforce , Startups November 4 Boston Buy One Pass, Get a Second Free Cover more ground at TechCrunch&#8217;s ultimate founder bootcamp of the year by bringing your co-founder or partner for free Book before the "
